INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Mandiant, Kaspersky, WIRED and CSA public reporting on 2023 3CX compromise", "source_url": "SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-008; SRC-002; SRC-009", "text": "Public evidence chain: Mandiant says the initial compromise vector of 3CX's network was malicious software downloaded from the Trading Technologies website, specifically a tampered X_TRADER installer from an earlier software supply-chain compromise. A 3CX employee installed the infected application; the attackers then moved laterally, harvested credentials, and compromised both Windows and macOS 3CX build environments. On Windows they deployed TAXHAUL and COLDCAT, with DLL search-order hijacking through IKEEXT and LocalSystem persistence; on macOS they used POOLRAT with Launch Daemons. The trojanized 3CX DesktopApp versions spread from 3CX's website and contained SUDDENICON, which obtained encrypted C2 URLs from icons in a GitHub repository; it then downloaded ICONICSTEALER, which stole browser information. Mandiant tracks the activity as UNC4736, a suspected North Korean nexus cluster, and assesses with moderate confidence that it is related to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity. Mandiant reports overlap with Trading Technologies compromise, AppleJeus-related infrastructure, and cryptocurrency/fintech targeting. Kaspersky reports Gopuram as a later/final modular backdoor in selected infections, with surgical targeting of cryptocurrency companies; it attributes the campaign to Lazarus with medium-to-high confidence based on Gopuram coexisting with AppleJeus, shared infrastructure, and related loader/shellcode patterns, while noting the shellcode is open-source and used by other actors. WIRED reports Mandiant's patient-zero reconstruction: a 3CX employee PC was compromised through the earlier Trading Technologies/X_TRADER supply-chain attack, access spread through 3CX's network to a software development server, and the 3CX installer was corrupted, infecting customers. WIRED also reports aliases/assessments Kimsuky, Emerald Sleet, or Velvet Chollima, but these are publicly presented as the hacker group's names/assessment and should not be treated as identical to Mandiant's UNC4736 without qualification. CSA Singapore confirms trojanized Windows and macOS DesktopApp versions and capabilities to harvest system information and steal browser data and stored credentials."}
